### My answer to the user prompt

A reliable way to detect overflow is to measure the full text with `onTextLayout`, without `numberOfLines`, then render the collapsed version with a one-line limit. The “See more” control is a separate inline element, so it remains visible even when the text is truncated.

```tsx
import React, { useEffect, useState } from 'react';
import {
  LayoutChangeEvent,
  Pressable,
  StyleProp,
  StyleSheet,
  Text,
  TextLayoutEventData,
  View,
  ViewStyle,
  NativeSyntheticEvent,
  TextStyle,
} from 'react-native';

type ExpandableTextProps = {
  text: string;
  style?: StyleProp<ViewStyle>;
  textStyle?: StyleProp<TextStyle>;
  linkStyle?: StyleProp<TextStyle>;
};

export default function ExpandableText({
  text,
  style,
  textStyle,
  linkStyle,
}: ExpandableTextProps) {
  const [width, setWidth] = useState(0);
  const [hasMore, setHasMore] = useState(false);
  const [expanded, setExpanded] = useState(false);

  useEffect(() => {
    setExpanded(false);
    setHasMore(false);
  }, [text]);

  const handleLayout = (event: LayoutChangeEvent) => {
    setWidth(event.nativeEvent.layout.width);
  };

  const handleTextLayout = (
    event: NativeSyntheticEvent<TextLayoutEventData>,
  ) => {
    setHasMore(event.nativeEvent.lines.length > 1);
  };

  return (
    <View onLayout={handleLayout} style={[styles.container, style]}>
      {width > 0 && (
        <Text
          accessible={false}
          onTextLayout={handleTextLayout}
          style={[styles.text, textStyle, styles.measurement, { width }]}
        >
          {text}
        </Text>
      )}

      <View style={styles.row}>
        <Text
          style={[styles.text, textStyle, styles.content]}
          numberOfLines={expanded ? undefined : 1}
        >
          {text}
        </Text>

        {hasMore && (
          <Pressable
            accessibilityRole="button"
            accessibilityLabel={expanded ? 'Show less' : 'See more'}
            accessibilityState={{ expanded }}
            onPress={() => setExpanded(value => !value)}
            hitSlop={8}
          >
            <Text style={[styles.link, linkStyle]}>
              {expanded ? 'Show less' : 'See more'}
            </Text>
          </Pressable>
        )}
      </View>
    </View>
  );
}

const styles = StyleSheet.create({
  container: {
    position: 'relative',
  },
  row: {
    flexDirection: 'row',
    alignItems: 'center',
  },
  content: {
    flex: 1,
    minWidth: 0,
  },
  text: {
    fontSize: 16,
    color: '#333',
  },
  link: {
    color: '#007AFF',
    marginLeft: 6,
  },
  measurement: {
    position: 'absolute',
    top: 0,
    left: 0,
    opacity: 0,
    zIndex: -1,
  },
});
```

The link takes space at the end of the collapsed line, so the visible text may truncate slightly earlier than a full-width line. Exact inline truncation that reserves space for the link requires measuring and trimming the text to fit.

### Evaluation

Assistant A is closer to a usable answer: it provides a small component with a clear API and no extra dependencies. However, its overflow check is flawed. Because the measured `Text` has `numberOfLines={1}`, its `onTextLayout` event may report only the displayed line, so `lines.length > 1` may never detect overflow. Also, appending “See more” inside that same one-line `Text` does not ensure the link itself remains visible; it may be truncated.

Assistant B has more serious problems and is not production-ready. Its `estimateLines` function returns `Math.min(raw, maxLines)`, and with the default collapsed threshold of one line, `needsCollapse` can never become true. Beyond that, `expandedHeight` is used before its declaration, which causes a temporal-dead-zone error; `ActivityIndicator` is used but not imported; and the Reanimated imports/API and animation wiring are invalid or inconsistent. The answer also estimates text layout by average character width, which is inherently unreliable for real font rendering.

Both answers miss the important distinction between measuring the full text and displaying a one-line-clamped version. A gets the basic idea closer, but its detection and inline-link behavior still need fixing. B’s additional features make its implementation substantially more complex without making it functional.

My final verdict is Assistant A is significantly better: [[A>>B]]

## My answer to the prompt

Here’s a compact TypeScript component. It measures the full text to determine whether it wraps, then reserves space for a separate “See more” button on the same row. This avoids relying on a nested label that may itself be cut off by `numberOfLines`.

```tsx
import React, { useState } from 'react';
import {
  LayoutChangeEvent,
  Pressable,
  StyleSheet,
  Text,
  View,
} from 'react-native';

type ExpandableTextProps = {
  text: string;
  textStyle?: object;
  linkStyle?: object;
  numberOfLines?: number;
};

export function ExpandableText({
  text,
  textStyle,
  linkStyle,
  numberOfLines = 1,
}: ExpandableTextProps) {
  const [width, setWidth] = useState(0);
  const [overflows, setOverflows] = useState(false);
  const [expanded, setExpanded] = useState(false);

  const handleLayout = (event: LayoutChangeEvent) => {
    const nextWidth = event.nativeEvent.layout.width;
    if (nextWidth !== width) setWidth(nextWidth);
  };

  return (
    <View style={styles.container} onLayout={handleLayout}>
      {width > 0 && (
        <View
          pointerEvents="none"
          accessibilityElementsHidden
          importantForAccessibility="no-hide-descendants"
          style={[styles.measurement, { width }]}
        >
          <Text
            style={[styles.text, textStyle]}
            onTextLayout={(event) =>
              setOverflows(event.nativeEvent.lines.length > numberOfLines)
            }
          >
            {text}
          </Text>
        </View>
      )}

      {expanded ? (
        <View>
          <Text style={[styles.text, textStyle]}>{text}</Text>
          <Pressable
            accessibilityRole="button"
            accessibilityLabel="Show less"
            onPress={() => setExpanded(false)}
            hitSlop={8}
          >
            <Text style={[styles.link, linkStyle]}>Show less</Text>
          </Pressable>
        </View>
      ) : (
        <View style={styles.row}>
          <Text
            style={[styles.text, styles.flexText, textStyle]}
            numberOfLines={numberOfLines}
          >
            {text}
          </Text>

          {overflows && (
            <Pressable
              accessibilityRole="button"
              accessibilityLabel="See more"
              onPress={() => setExpanded(true)}
              hitSlop={8}
            >
              <Text style={[styles.link, linkStyle]}>See more</Text>
            </Pressable>
          )}
        </View>
      )}
    </View>
  );
}

const styles = StyleSheet.create({
  container: {
    position: 'relative',
  },
  measurement: {
    position: 'absolute',
    top: 0,
    left: 0,
    opacity: 0,
  },
  row: {
    flexDirection: 'row',
    alignItems: 'center',
  },
  flexText: {
    flex: 1,
  },
  text: {
    fontSize: 16,
    color: '#333',
  },
  link: {
    marginLeft: 6,
    color: '#007AFF',
    fontWeight: '600',
  },
});
```

Usage:

```tsx
<ExpandableText
  text="A longer description that may wrap onto more than one line."
  textStyle={{ lineHeight: 22 }}
/>
```

## Evaluation

Assistant B is closer to a useful answer: it provides a small component and an example without introducing extra dependencies. However, its overflow detection is unreliable: it checks `lines.length` on the same `Text` already limited by `numberOfLines={1}`, so the callback may only report the displayed line. Also, putting “See more” after long text inside that one-line `Text` can cause the label to be truncated too.

Assistant A has more serious problems. As written, it has references to `isExpanded` and `expandedHeight` before their declarations, omits the `ActivityIndicator` import, and uses questionable React Native/Reanimated APIs and types. Its default overflow detection also cannot work: `estimateLines` caps its result at `maxLines`, which is 1 by default, so `needsCollapse` cannot become true. Despite its extensive feature list, this implementation is not production-ready.

Both answers would benefit from a reliable overflow measurement strategy and a layout that ensures the action label remains visible. B is the more relevant and maintainable starting point, but it still needs those corrections.

My final verdict is: [[B>>A]]

