## Evaluation

Assistant B is closer to a useful answer: it provides a small component and an example without introducing extra dependencies. However, its overflow detection is unreliable: it checks `lines.length` on the same `Text` already limited by `numberOfLines={1}`, so the callback may only report the displayed line. Also, putting “See more” after long text inside that one-line `Text` can cause the label to be truncated too.

Assistant A has more serious problems. As written, it has references to `isExpanded` and `expandedHeight` before their declarations, omits the `ActivityIndicator` import, and uses questionable React Native/Reanimated APIs and types. Its default overflow detection also cannot work: `estimateLines` caps its result at `maxLines`, which is 1 by default, so `needsCollapse` cannot become true. Despite its extensive feature list, this implementation is not production-ready.

Both answers would benefit from a reliable overflow measurement strategy and a layout that ensures the action label remains visible. B is the more relevant and maintainable starting point, but it still needs those corrections.

